FORMATTING TWICE GIVES WHAT FORMATTING ONCE GAVE.

`then-args` runs `format` again over the first run's output, so `expected.md`
is the SECOND pass. It is byte for byte what a single pass produces, which is
the claim: the output is a fixed point.

This is the case that would have caught the defect at its worst. While the
continuation was written as a bullet, a run turned a paragraph into an item,
and that item was then a real item to the next run. The damage was not a
formatting choice made afresh each time; it accumulated in the file. A one
pass case can only say that a shape looks wrong. This one says that running
the tool again does not make it worse, which is the property anyone piping
`md format` over a directory is relying on.

Idempotence is also the cheapest test of whether the writer and the reader
agree. They agree only if what the writer emits is read back as the same
model, and here that means the reader must see `  Tail paragraph...` as the
first item's continuation and not as a third bullet.
